id: k_timer_start
title: Start a periodic timer
category: Timers
version: 4.4.0
aliases: start timer|periodic timer|run every second|schedule timer|timer tick|tick every second|repeating timer
signature: void k_timer_start(struct k_timer *timer, k_timeout_t duration, k_timeout_t period);
source: https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/v4.4.0/include/zephyr/kernel.h#L1971-L1972
related: K_TIMER_DEFINE|k_timer_init|k_timer_stop|k_timer_status_sync
## Explanation
Start a Zephyr kernel timer. The first timeout controls the initial expiration; a nonzero period repeats. This example waits for timer expirations from a thread, instead of running slow work in a timer expiry callback.
## Parameters
timer: pointer to an initialized timer, for example one declared by K_TIMER_DEFINE.
duration: first expiration delay, such as K_MSEC(250).
period: repeat interval, or K_NO_WAIT for a one-shot timer.
Returns: nothing (void).
## Example
#include <zephyr/kernel.h>

K_TIMER_DEFINE(heartbeat, NULL, NULL);

int main(void)
{
    k_timer_start(&heartbeat, K_MSEC(100), K_SECONDS(1));
    while (1) {
        uint32_t expirations = k_timer_status_sync(&heartbeat);
        printk("Timer expirations since last check: %u\n", expirations);
    }
    return 0;
}
## Requirements
Assumptions: a Zephyr kernel application with kernel timers and a working system clock. No additional Devicetree pins are required. The example uses a kernel timer, not a particular SoC hardware timer peripheral.
For printed output, enable CONFIG_PRINTK and a suitable console in the project/board configuration if they are not already enabled.
## Mistakes
Do not assume a timer callback is a normal thread: expiry functions execute in the system clock interrupt context and must not sleep or block. Starting an already running timer resets its timing/status. K_FOREVER as the initial duration does not start counting. k_timer_status_sync() waits and therefore belongs in thread context.
